Chapter 1 — Foundation Concepts
Before understanding the vulnerability you need to understand four basic concepts. Everything else builds on these.
What is the Linux Kernel?
Think of a computer like a company.
Applications (Chrome, Python scripts) = Employees
Operating System = Management layer
Linux Kernel = The CEO
Hardware (CPU, RAM, Disk) = The factory floor
The kernel is the most powerful software on the system. It controls everything — memory, CPU, file access, network. When the kernel does something, no other software can stop it or question it.
This is why kernel vulnerabilities are so dangerous. If an attacker controls the kernel, they control everything.
What is Privilege Escalation?
Normal user (you) → can read your own files
Root user → can read, write, delete EVERYTHING
Privilege escalation is going from normal user to root without permission.
CVE-2026-31431 is a local privilege escalation vulnerability in the Linux kernel that allows an unprivileged user on an affected system to escalate to root. TryHackMe
What is the Page Cache?
When Linux reads a file from disk — it stores a copy in RAM for speed. This RAM copy is called the page cache.
File on disk: /usr/bin/su (setuid binary)
         ↓
Linux reads it into RAM
         ↓
Page cache: in-memory copy of the file
         ↓
Every program that runs /usr/bin/su
uses this cached copy
Key insight: If you can modify the page cache — you change what programs execute — without touching the disk file at all.
The flaw allows a local user to modify the kernel's cached copy of a file in memory without changing the file on disk. Because the modification exists only in the page cache, the underlying file on disk remains unchanged. Standard disk forensics would not detect the alteration. CyberDefenders
What is a Setuid Binary?
Some programs need to run with root privileges even when a normal user executes them. These are called setuid binaries.
/usr/bin/su     — switch user (needs root)
/usr/bin/sudo   — run as superuser (needs root)
/usr/bin/passwd — change password (needs root)
When you run su — even as a normal user — it temporarily runs as root to do its job. If you corrupt the page cache copy of su — you control what root executes.

Chapter 2 — The Vulnerability Explained Simply
What is AF_ALG?
AF_ALG is a socket type that exposes the kernel's crypto subsystem to unprivileged userspace. A user can open a socket, bind to any AEAD template, and invoke encryption or decryption on arbitrary data. No privileges required. TryHackMe
In simple terms — AF_ALG lets normal users ask the kernel to encrypt or decrypt data. This is legitimate and useful. The problem is in how one specific part of it handles memory.
What is splice()?
splice() transfers data between file descriptors and pipes without copying, passing page cache pages by reference. When a user splices a file into a pipe and then into an AF_ALG socket, the socket's input scatterlist holds direct references to the kernel's cached pages of that file. TryHackMe
In simple terms — splice() is a fast way to move data. Instead of copying data, it just shares a reference to the same memory. This is the efficiency feature that created the vulnerability.
The Root Cause — Simple Explanation
Copy Fail is a logic bug in the Linux kernel's authencesn cryptographic template. It lets an unprivileged local user trigger a deterministic, controlled 4-byte write into the page cache of any readable file on the system. TryHackMe
Break that down:
Logic bug = not a memory corruption, a logical mistake
authencesn = a specific encryption template in the kernel
4-byte write = can write exactly 4 bytes anywhere
page cache of any readable file = target any file
The attacker can write 4 bytes into the in-memory copy of any file they can read. Since setuid binaries are readable by everyone — they become the target.
Why is 4 bytes enough?
4 bytes sounds tiny. But in a compiled binary — 4 bytes at the right location can change a conditional jump instruction. Change one jump — change whether the program checks permissions or not. Change that check — get root.

Chapter 3 — The Attack Chain Step by Step
Phase 5: By corrupting kernel structures associated with credentials or execution context, the attacker escalates their process to UID 0. This completes the transition from unprivileged user to full root without touching the network. At this point, kernel trust boundaries are broken, SELinux/AppArmor protections are effectively neutralized, and local security controls are bypassed. TryHackMe
Here is the complete attack chain in plain language:
Step 1 — Attacker has local access
(shell account, compromised service,
malicious container, CI/CD runner)

Step 2 — Open AF_ALG socket
No privileges needed — any user can do this

Step 3 — splice() a setuid binary into the socket
/usr/bin/su is readable by everyone
splice() gives kernel a reference to its page cache

Step 4 — Trigger the authencesn scratch write
The crypto template writes to the scatterlist
which now points to the page cache of su

Step 5 — 4-byte write lands in su's page cache
The in-memory copy of su is now modified
The disk file is untouched

Step 6 — Execute modified su
su runs as root (it is setuid)
But the code it executes is now attacker-controlled
Root shell obtained
What makes this different from previous bugs?
Dirty Cow required winning a race condition in the VM subsystem's copy-on-write path. It often needed multiple attempts and sometimes crashed the system. Dirty Pipe was version-specific and required precise pipe buffer manipulation. Copy Fail is a straight-line logic flaw. It triggers without races, retries, or crash-prone timing windows. TryHackMe
This means:

No timing tricks needed
Works every single time
Does not crash the system
Same script works on every Linux distribution

The entire exploit is a short Python script using only standard library modules. No compiled payloads, no dependency installation. TryHackMe

Chapter 4 — Why Cloud and Kubernetes Makes It Critical
On a normal Linux server
Attacker needs: a shell account on the machine. If SSH is locked down and no users can log in — risk is low.
On Kubernetes — completely different story
In a typical Kubernetes cluster, containerised workloads run on worker nodes. Any container that can exec into a pod on a node has process-level access on that node. Multi-tenant clusters where multiple teams or applications share worker nodes are the worst-case scenario: one compromised or malicious tenant container can escalate to node root. TryHackMe
In simple terms:
Kubernetes cluster has many containers
All containers share the same worker node (Linux host)
If one container is compromised —
attacker runs exploit from inside container —
escalates to ROOT of the entire worker node —
now controls ALL containers on that node —
can read all secrets, tokens, passwords
of every other application on the node
On Kubernetes worker nodes, successful exploitation can give an attacker full node control, access to pod-mounted secrets, service account tokens, and pivot paths toward other workloads or the control plane. LetsDefend
The blast radius in Kubernetes
One compromised container
        ↓
Copy Fail exploit runs
        ↓
Root on worker node
        ↓
Access to ALL pods on that node
        ↓
Steal service account tokens
        ↓
Use tokens to access Kubernetes API
        ↓
Potentially compromise entire cluster
        ↓
Access to all secrets across all namespaces
This is why your trainer called it cloud and Kubernetes privilege escalation — the local privilege escalation becomes a full cluster compromise.

Chapter 5 — Affected Systems
The vulnerability affects virtually all Linux distributions running kernels released from 2017 until patched versions are applied, including Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Amazon Linux 2023, Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.1, SUSE 16, Debian, Fedora, and Arch Linux. The CVSS score is 7.8 (High). TryHackMe
